recv_files logic overview  
- The function enters a loop to read file-transfer requests from the input channel.  
- For each file entry it looks up the corresponding local file and remote attributes, then decides how to handle the transfer (skip, log, or apply a literal transfer).  
- It computes an on-disk filename and performs checks against download filters.  
- It opens or creates a temporary (or backup) output file, then copies data from the remote input to the temporary file.  
- After copying, it invokes routines to finalize (renaming, setting permissions, hard-links or partial directories), then closes descriptors and cleans up temporary files.  
- Throughout, it updates status counters, optionally batches messages, and handles phase 1/2 bookkeeping.  

Special handling and pitfalls  
- The filename buffer (fbuf) and auxiliary buffers (xname, fnamecmpbuf) are all sized to MAXPATHLEN.  
- String concatenation functions (pathjoin, stringjoin) callers never trust the length of user-controlled path segments; they use the provided buffer size parameter.  
- The code carefully avoids shell‐injection or command‐injection vectors here, since all opens, renames, and message writes happen through safe system calls or built-in routines.  
- Unchecked “user” input is inserted as a path component into the local filename; because fbuf is never overrun, and because path-length limits are enforced elsewhere in the protocol, a malicious remote path component never overruns the buffer.  
- File‐metadata and hard‐link operations avoid special cases where a directory is mistakenly treated as a regular file (the special handling for directories under fd1) prevents overwriting a directory name with a file.  
- Control of file‐attribute preservation, permissions, and hard‐links is done in an explicit check.  
- Error paths are consistently guarded, with cleanup calls and message discards on any open/close or file‐I/O failure.  

Review in short  
- All buffer lengths or data sizes used in path‐construction routines include an explicit size parameter that can be used to detect overflow.  
- Input path components come from a protocol exchange, but because they flow through bounds‐checked routines for assembly into local_names, they cannot overflow.  
- Special handling for directory names prevents a renaming of a directory into a file slot.  
- Error conditions and cleanup paths prevent file descriptor leaks or half‐opened files.  
- There is no evidence of format‐string use, system or shell invocation based on untrusted input, or integer truncation leading to path overflows.

I find no remaining unchecked memory or I/O operations or other security‐relevant bugs.

